WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Glad you're with us on First Principles Daily, episode 7. Closing the week with one more

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.600
look at where the next 10X is hiding. ammonia fertilizer that sells for hundreds of dollars

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.780
a ton rests on raw inputs whose theoretical floor is a small fraction of that price.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.000
ammonia that ends up on farm fields today carries a delimbred price that can exceed several

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.720
hundred dollars per metric ton. The atoms themselves come from air and ultimately from water or natural

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:35.080
gas. Between those two points lies a chain of high temperature, high pressure steps, centralized

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.900
plans, and fossil-derived hydrogen whose cumulative markups produce a large gap between finished

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:46.460
cost and the physical minimum. That gap is the opening for First Principles Examination.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:53.300
The Haber Bosch process commercialized in the 1910s fixes nitrogen by reacting it with hydrogen

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:59.860
at roughly 200 atmospheres and 400-500 degrees Celsius over an iron catalyst.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:06.400
Those operating conditions were not arbitrary. At the time, the only way to push the equilibrium

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.940
far enough toward ammonia was to use thick-walled steel vessels that could safely contain the pressure

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.520
and an iron catalyst whose activity only became useful once the temperature climbed into that range.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.800
The design choices that became standard were driven by the metallurgy and energy sources available

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:30.560
at the time. Large compressors, thick-walled vessels, and a steady supply of hydrogen made by steam

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.920
reforming natural gas set the pattern. Once those plans were built, incremental improvements focused

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.340
on heat recovery and larger single-trained capacities rather than on questioning the central

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:48.660
architecture itself. A natural objection is that decades of refinement must have already squeezed

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.820
most of the waste out. Yet the architecture itself, centralized high pressure and natural gas

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:00.360
dependent, remained untouched because the capital was already sunk and the permits already granted.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.940
Natural gas price swings therefore translate directly into ammonia price swings because roughly

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:14.920
70-80% of the hydrogen feedstock still comes from methane. A typical modern plant consumes on the order

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:22.520
of 30-35 gigajoules of energy per ton of ammonia produced. To put that in perspective, 30 gigajoules

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:29.460
is roughly 8.3 megawatt hours. If most of that energy is locked inside the hydrogen molecule rather

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.780
than used only for the synthesis loop, then every dollar move in natural gas price moves the ammonia

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:42.140
cost line almost in lock step. The majority of that energy sits inside the hydrogen rather than in

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:47.680
the synthesis loop alone. That energy intensity, combined with the capital cost of the high-pressure

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:54.160
equipment, sets a baseline-delivered price. The price has historically ranged from roughly $300

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:01.460
to more than $800 per ton, depending on region and feedstock costs. One might ask whether simply

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:07.340
building still larger plants could dilute those fixed costs further, but the industry already builds

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:13.480
at the scale that spreads compressor and reformer costs across millions of tons. Beyond that point,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.880
additional size mainly adds pipeline and port complexity rather than proportional savings.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.800
The industry's scale reinforces the pattern. Plants are built at 1-2 million tons per year to

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:31.880
spread the fixed costs of compressors, reformers, and ammonia synthesis loops. Once permitted and

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:37.920
financed, these facilities operate for decades. The engineering assumptions of the 1920s through

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.700
1970s therefore remain embedded in today's cost structure. Transportation adds another layer.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:51.580
ammonia is liquefied or converted to urea before it travels. The logistics of moving millions of

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:58.200
tons from a handful of coastal or pipeline adjacent sites to inland farms compound of the final price.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:05.300
Regulation and financing further widen the spread. New plant construction faces multi-year environmental

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:11.540
reviews, safety standards written for large centralized inventories, and financing models that

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:18.180
assume 20-30 years of steady natural gas prices. Each of these frictions is treated as an unavoidable

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:23.520
cost of doing business rather than a variable that might be attacked by changing the underlying

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.540
process or scale. An obvious pushback is that safety rules exist for good reason. The

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:34.540
counter is that those rules were written around the hazard profile of million ton inventories.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.560
So any redesign that shrinks inventory size must also renegotiate the regulatory premise,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:47.260
not merely the hardware. A rough magic wand estimate begins with the atoms and the thermodynamics.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.800
Nitrogen is free from air. The stoichiometric hydrogen requirement for one ton of ammonia is

00:04:53.800 --> 00:05:00.300
approximately 180 kilograms. If that hydrogen is produced by electrolysis at the theoretical

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:07.660
minimum of 39.4 kilowatt hours per kilogram of hydrogen. The electricity demand is on the order of

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:13.840
seven megawatt hours per ton of ammonia before any process losses. Adding the roughly five to

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.580
six gigajoules or about 1.5 megawatt hours needed to drive the synthesis reaction itself at its

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:27.080
thermodynamic limit brings the combined floor to something like 8.5 to 9 megawatt hours per ton.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:34.040
At electricity prices of 25 to 45 dollars per megawatt hour, that energy floor sits at roughly

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:40.900
200 to 400 dollars per ton. That figure is only an energy floor. It leaves out real-world

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.620
electrolysis efficiency, compression work, and the cost of any equipment. Compare that floor with

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:53.340
observed market prices. When ammonia trades near 500 dollars per ton, the ratio between finished price

00:05:53.340 --> 00:06:00.680
and the energy only estimate is already between 1.25 and 2.5. If one includes the capital recovery

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:06.400
and operating costs embedded in today's plants, the effective idiot index climbs higher still.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.320
The precise ratio is not a fixed measurement because both electricity prices and ammonia prices

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.200
move. Yet the order of magnitude reveals that the dominant expenses lie in the conversion steps

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:25.060
rather than in the scarcity of the atoms. Those conversion steps are concrete. Steam reforming of natural

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:31.660
gas accounts for the largest share of energy and carbon intensity. The subsequent compression to 200

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:38.440
bar requires large, expensive machinery. The synthesis loop must continuously purge inerts and

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:44.600
manage heat. The final product must be refrigerated or converted to a more stable form for storage

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:50.440
and transport. Each of these stages adds capital, maintenance, and energy overhead that the

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:56.480
magic wand calculation simply omits. The gap therefore lives in the centralized, high-pressure,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.520
fossil hydrogen architecture rather than in any fundamental shortage of nitrogen or hydrogen atoms.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:08.520
A redesign would start by separating hydrogen production from the synthesis plant. If low cost

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:14.120
renewable electricity can drive electrolysis at scale, the hydrogen cost becomes an electricity

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:20.620
cost rather than a natural gas cost. The next move would be to relax the pressure and temperature

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:27.120
requirements. Newer catalysts or electrochemical routes that operate nearer to ambient conditions could

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.740
shrink the vessels and compressors. That change would lower both capital intensity and the minimum

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:39.540
economic plant size. Smaller, modular synthesis units could then be cited closer to renewable power

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:45.980
and to demand. Cutting transport and storage markups would follow. For any of these moves to close the

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:52.700
gap, three conditions must hold simultaneously. Electrolyzer capital cost must continue to fall so

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:58.900
that hydrogen reaches the one to two dollars per kilogram range on an all-in-basus. Catalysts must

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:04.780
achieve acceptable rates at lower pressure without prohibitive rare metal loadings. Permitting

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:10.500
regimes must accommodate distributed ammonia plants whose hazard profiles differ from today's

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:16.840
one million ton facilities. The hard problems are real. Intermittent power requires either storage

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:23.080
or flexible operation that today's haver loops are not designed for. Nitrogen separation from air

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:29.340
still costs energy. Any new catalyst must survive thousands of hours without rapid deactivation.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.500
None of these barriers is dismissed by pointing to the magic wand number. They are the engineering

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:41.940
surfaces that must be attacked next. A product whose price greatly exceeds the cost of its constituent

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:48.560
atoms and minimum energy is signaling a design or process problem, not a raw material shortage.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:54.820
Questioning the requirement for 200 bar centralized plants before optimizing the compressors inside

00:08:54.820 --> 00:09:01.460
them is the faster route to lower cost. Tomorrow, we examine another domain where the same gap between

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:07.580
floor and finish price invites the same scrutiny. That's first principles daily for today. If this

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:12.360
changed how you see what things cost, share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:18.640
you listen. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, one example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:25.680
